  "Question": "If \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z\\) are real numbers such that \\[ \\begin{aligned} (x-3)^2+(y-4)^2\\\\ {}+(z-5)^2&=0, \\end{aligned} \\] then \\(x+y+z=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-12\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(8\\)",
    "D": "\\(12\\)",
    "E": "\\(50\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Every square of a real number is nonnegative. If any of the three squared terms were positive, their sum would be positive. Since their sum is zero, all three must be zero:\n\\[\n(x-3)^2=(y-4)^2=(z-5)^2=0.\n\\]\nA square equals zero only when its base is zero, so \\(x=3\\), \\(y=4\\), and \\(z=5\\). These values satisfy the original equation. Hence\n\\[\nx+y+z=3+4+5=12,\n\\]\nchoice D.",
